COMMAND

  BINCV_PI_OPENCV=1 ./scripts/run_on_pi.sh pi4 \
      './benchmark/denoise_benchmark > denoise_benchmark.log'

  BINCV_PI_OPENCV=1 is not optional: the denominator is an OpenCV call and the
  device's default build is core-only.

================================================================================
HOW TO READ THE LADDER -- two different effects, and only one of them is cache
================================================================================

THE 640x480 RATIO IS SUBSTANTIALLY A CACHE RESULT. The Pi 4's L2 is 1 MiB,
shared. At 640x480 the reference implementation holds 2150400 B live and does not
fit; binCV's fused call holds 76800 B and does. The evidence is inside the
baseline rather than across the two sides: the SAME OpenCV code costs 3.44739
ns/pixel at 640x480 and 1.46580 ns/pixel at 320x240 -- 2.4x SLOWER per pixel on
the larger frame -- which is the signature of a working set crossing L2, and the
same mechanism EXPERIMENTS.md X-6 identified for the logic kernels.

THE SMALL-SIZE RATIOS ARE NOT. Below 320x240 the baseline's per-pixel cost RISES
again (1.47 -> 2.26 -> 3.64 ns/pixel) while its working set FALLS (537600 ->
134400 -> 39480 B), all of it comfortably inside L2. No cache explanation
predicts that. What does: the reference makes EIGHT cv:: calls per frame, and
this run measures their fixed per-call cost directly at 4.147 us -- 20% of the
94x60 frame, 10% of 160x120, 4% of 320x240 and 0.4% of 640x480. Subtracting even
that leaves a per-pixel cost still rising down the ladder (1.41 -> 2.05 -> 2.91
ns/pixel), so the remainder is per-frame and per-row overhead in those eight
calls rather than the operation.

  CONSEQUENCE, and it is the reason this section exists: THE RATIOS AT DIFFERENT
  SIZES ARE NOT COMPARABLE WITH EACH OTHER, and the pyramid-level ratios are not
  a stronger claim than the 640x480 one -- they are a weaker one, with more
  overhead in the denominator. The number quotable for T3.1 is the 640x480 row,
  with the working-set column beside it.

THE DENOMINATOR IS THE LEANER OF THE TWO OpenCV ROWS. The reference builds seven
cv::Mats with cv::Mat::zeros; four of them are completely overwritten by the
cv::min/cv::max that follows, so their zero-fill is dead work. Both spellings are
timed. 'OpenCV CV_8U' pays only the two zero-fills that ARE the border (the last
column of `right` and the first row of `above` are never written by the copyTo
calls); 'OpenCV as-written' pays all six, and is 1.5x to 2.0x slower down the
ladder. Every ratio here is against the FASTER one, i.e. conservative by that
whole gap.

RUN-TO-RUN SPREAD, so a later reader does not over-read a small difference. An
earlier run of this same binary on this same device gave 56.36x / 23.09x / 33.45x
/ 26.49x for fused uint32 against 56.76x / 22.75x / 30.63x / 28.82x here. The
640x480 and 320x240 rows repeat to within 2%; the two smallest sizes move by up
to 10%, which is what a frame whose denominator is a fifth per-call overhead
looks like. The uint64-versus-uint32 margin at 160x120 changes SIGN between the
two runs, so nothing should be concluded from it (E-9 holds that question).

================================================================================
T3.1 denoise -- three-pixel median vs the reference implementation
================================================================================

DENOMINATOR (ARCHITECTURE 10.3): SEAL's three_pix_median_filter on the SAME
binary content stored as CV_8U -- cv::min/cv::max over two zero-filled
neighbour matrices, ported call for call. That is what the pipeline runs
today without binCV.

binCV rows: the fused one-pass kernel (ops/denoise.hpp) at uint32 and uint64,
and the composed shiftDown + shiftLeft + majority3 spelling it replaced --
same image, three passes, two frame-sized scratch buffers.

TWO OpenCV ROWS. 'OpenCV CV_8U' re-zeroes only the two neighbour matrices,
whose zeros ARE the border; 'OpenCV as-written' also re-zeroes the four
temporaries the reference builds with cv::Mat::zeros and then completely
overwrites. The DENOMINATOR is the first, so every ratio is conservative by
the gap between them.

The checksum column folds EVERY destination pixel and is representation-
independent, so all rows of a size must print the same value.

Working set is one call's live buffers, not a per-buffer ratio (CLAUDE.md,
ARCHITECTURE 10.4).

FIXED PER-CALL COST (hazard 5), measured on a 2x2 frame -- the part of every
row below that is not the operation:
  OpenCV reference (8 cv:: calls):     4.147 us per call
  binCV denoiseMedian3:                0.014 us per call
  Divided by a frame's pixel count this is what a small frame's ns/pixel is
  mostly made of. It is NOT subtracted from the tables; it is printed beside
  them so the ladder is read with it.

